GCF columnar format (internal/gcf):
The GCF "generic profile" text format implemented in internal/gcf originates
from gcf-typescript, Copyright (c) Blackwell Systems
(https://github.com/blackwell-systems/gcf-typescript), licensed under the MIT
License, as vendored into OmniRoute
(https://github.com/diegosouzapw/OmniRoute), also MIT-licensed. internal/gcf
is an independent Go reimplementation of the format (scalars-only subset);
no upstream source code was copied.

Compose templates adapted from hyperframes-student-kit (render/compose-templates):
stat-card, section-title, callout-label and checklist-card are adapted from card
designs in hyperframes-student-kit, pinned at commit 0d30152, which is licensed
under the MIT License with an additional use permission for its style library
and templates. Each template is a rewrite in CSS keyframes of the card's layout
and motion; no upstream source file was copied. The kit's licence texts are kept
verbatim in render/compose-templates/_third_party/hyperframes-student-kit/, whose
PROVENANCE.md records the source repository, the pinned commit, and what was
taken and what was not.

ComfyUI workflow template fixtures (render/testdata/templates-catalog/):
The workflow files there are trimmed excerpts of templates from Comfy-Org/workflow_templates
(https://github.com/Comfy-Org/workflow_templates), Copyright (c) 2023-present Comfy Org,
licensed under the MIT License. The licence text is kept beside them in
LICENSE-comfy-workflow-templates.txt. They are test inputs only and are not shipped.

ContextBench task generators and graders (internal/compeval/contextbench_*.go):
The four task generators (kv_store, log_triage, needle_retention, sudoku_sketchpad),
the four graders, the standalone-marker and operation-rendering rules, and the table of
published levels are ported to Go from ContextBench
(https://github.com/RulinShao/ContextBench), licensed under the Apache License,
Version 2.0, pinned at commit fb533c18c78a82d3f1b2047a9c1700735cf829ae. The operation
mix is ported: the logic, the data tables (word lists, level knobs) and the texts of the
streamed operations (sentinel lines, headers, request and move wording) are carried over,
byte for byte where the stream is concerned, so that a seed produces the same task
stream; the Go code and comments are written for this repository. Each ported file names
the upstream file it comes from. The upstream's documentation, instruction scaffolds and
dataset are not copied, and no upstream data is committed. The upstream repository has
no NOTICE file at the pinned commit, its source files carry no copyright header, and its
LICENSE carries only the unfilled appendix line ("Copyright [yyyy] [name of copyright owner]").

Search-results compaction rung (internal/agent/rung_search.go):
The idea of thinning search results by relevance to the task (BM25 ranking, a cap per
file, diversity across files, a boost for error lines) is documented by the Headroom
project, Copyright 2025 Headroom Contributors (https://github.com/headroomlabs-ai/headroom),
licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. The caps used here (30 lines in total, 5 per
file, 15 files) are chosen to match the values shown in the upstream configuration example
of SearchCompressorConfig on its documentation page docs/content/docs/text-and-logs.mdx
(https://github.com/headroomlabs-ai/headroom/blob/main/docs/content/docs/text-and-logs.mdx).
internal/agent/rung_search.go is an independent, clean-room Go implementation written from
that documented behaviour; no upstream source code was read or copied. The rung is off by default.

Log-collapse compaction rung (internal/agent/rung_logs.go):
The idea of compressing build and test output while keeping the failures,
errors and boundary lines is the documented behavior of the Headroom project
(https://github.com/headroomlabs-ai/headroom, https://docs.headroomlabs.ai), licensed
under the Apache License, Version 2.0. internal/agent/rung_logs.go is a clean-room
implementation of that documented idea: the line-matching rule, the folding of runs,
the kept windows, the markers and the footer are this repository's own design, and no
Headroom source code was read or copied.

NVIDIA Xid catalog data (internal/gpuactivity/xidcatalog.go):
The Xid codes, mnemonics, descriptions and resolution buckets in xidcatalog.go, and the
meaning of each bucket, are taken from NVIDIA's Xid catalog (Xid-Catalog.xlsx, published
with "Analyzing the Xid Catalog" at https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/xid-errors/),
Copyright (c) NVIDIA CORPORATION, as embedded in NVIDIA NVSentinel
(https://github.com/NVIDIA/NVSentinel), licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0,
at release v1.28.0, file health-monitors/syslog-health-monitor/pkg/common/Xid-Catalog.xlsx
(sha256 7f70ce9684be0c9d98a367770341ccc1d8bca4465cebe42dab7e23d57df5d5a5). Only the used
rows of the Xids sheet and the bucket texts those rows name are carried, as Go data; the
workbook itself is not committed. The burst window (3 minutes) and the repeat rule (the
same Xid in 5 or more bursts on one GPU within 24 hours, Xid 31 and 45 excepted) applied
in internal/gpuactivity/faults.go are taken from NVSentinel's health-events-analyzer
rules; the Go code is written for this repository and no NVSentinel source code was copied.
